// WRC with a data dependency in the middle thread and an address dependency in
// the last -- the rmm-zoo ARMv8-vs-ARM ordering witness, at C level.
//
// rmm-zoo property: mca (Atomicity / Multicopy atomic).
// Source: Pulte et al., "Simplifying ARM Concurrency: Multicopy-atomic Axiomatic
//         and Operational Models for ARMv8" (POPL 2018); Sarkar et al., PLDI 2011.
//         Vendored shape: rmm-zoo litmus/strictly-weaker/ARMv8-vs-ARM/WRC+addrs.
//
// Both intra-thread reorderings are ruled out by dependencies, so seeing
// P1:x=1, P2:y=1 and P2:x=0 means P0's write reached P1 before it reached P2:
// the two threads did not see the writes in the same order. That is exactly a
// failure of multicopy atomicity.
//
// Reference verdicts: ARMv7 / POWER (non-MCA) ALLOW.
//                     ARMv8, RVWMO, x86-TSO, SC (MCA) FORBID.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  x := 1
} ||| {
  r1 := x;
  y := r1
} ||| {
  r2 := y;
  rq := &x;
  r3 := *(rq + (r2 - r2))
}
%%
allow (r1 = 1 && r2 = 1 && r3 = 0) [Power]
